From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Er Gibbio <gibbio@pm.me>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 20:42:09 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 24/24] display: let own-clock stream reports ride out network
 jitter

On its own clock, video reports the margin the default delay gives: 50 ms,
less by however much later than the target a frame arrives (0023).
spice-server's GStreamer rate control lowers the bit rate on any fall in
margin while it is below one frame interval of the guest's frame rate as
the server estimates it, and a new stream's first estimate can be low for
5 s (12 fps for a video at 29): falls of 1-7 ms took the limit down four
steps and one of 13 ms halved it, 19.7 to 7.9 Mbit/s in 4 s. Against 50
as the largest margin, a fall of 13 ms lowers the bit rate by a quarter
even at 30 fps, and one of 17 ms halves it. On Wi-Fi, frames arrived 1-5
ms later than the target in 7% of the reports, up to 13 ms in 23 min.

Leave a frame up to 20 ms later than the target out of the reports. The
server's model, replayed on those 23 min of reports, then takes no
decision at all; later frames still lower the margin, and the margin
still never exceeds 50, which keeps the server's audio rule (less bit
rate when the audio margin falls below half its peak and well under the
video margin) out of play.
---
 src/channel-display.c | 15 +++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 13 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/src/channel-display.c b/src/channel-display.c
index 334810d..f8d8631 100644
--- a/src/channel-display.c
+++ b/src/channel-display.c
@@ -66,6 +66,9 @@
 
 /* SPICE_STREAM_PLAYOUT_MS's default: see display_playout_shift() */
 #define PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS 50
+/* How much later than the target a frame may arrive before stream reports
+ * show it: see display_handle_stream_data() */
+#define PLAYOUT_REPORT_JITTER_MS 20
 /* How late a frame may still be shown while video follows the audio: see
  * stream_get_late_tolerance() */
 #define PLAYOUT_AUDIO_LATE_MS 100
@@ -2275,7 +2278,14 @@ static void display_handle_stream_data(SpiceChannel *channel, SpiceMsgIn *in)
      *   clock count there: while audio plays it runs the audio's delay behind,
      *   and its margin follows the audio buffer (~140 ms, 186 after a Wi-Fi
      *   pause grew it): reported, it lowered the bit rate twice as the audio
-     *   started and halved it at the pause, within 6 minutes;
+     *   started and halved it at the pause, within 6 minutes. Nor does a
+     *   frame arriving up to PLAYOUT_REPORT_JITTER_MS later than the target
+     *   (Wi-Fi jitter: 1-5 ms in 7% of the reports, up to 13 in 23 min): a
+     *   fall in margin of 1 ms lowers the bit rate while the server's estimate
+     *   of the guest's frame rate is below 20 fps, as a new stream's first one
+     *   can be for 5 s (12 fps for a video at 29), and against 50 one of 13 ms
+     *   lowers it by a quarter even at 30 fps; a stream went from 19.7 to 7.9
+     *   Mbit/s in 4 s;
      * - following the audio, the margin is about the audio's delay, which
      *   changes with the output device (~280 ms with AirPods, ~130 built in)
      *   and peaks for a moment when it does: from 408 to 131 ms, the server
@@ -2286,7 +2296,8 @@ static void display_handle_stream_data(SpiceChannel *channel, SpiceMsgIn *in)
     if (st->video_decoder->codec_type == SPICE_VIDEO_CODEC_TYPE_MJPEG) {
         server_margin = MAX(margin_report, margin);
     } else if (c->playout_target > 0 && !c->playout_audio) {
-        server_margin = MIN(margin - c->playout_target, 0) + PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS;
+        server_margin = MIN(margin - c->playout_target + PLAYOUT_REPORT_JITTER_MS, 0) +
+                        PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS;
     } else {
         server_margin = MIN(MAX(margin_report, margin), PLAYOUT_DEFAULT_MS);
         /* a frame late by no more than the decoder still shows is no drop */
